WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:03.200
-Our next guest is a
comedian, actor, and author.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.033
You know him from his work on
"Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:05.033 --> 00:00:06.334
"Trainwreck",
as well as his shows

00:00:06.334 --> 00:00:08.534
"Unconstitutional" and
"Red State Blue State."

00:00:08.534 --> 00:00:12.167
His latest show, "Colin Quinn:
The Last Best Hope,"

00:00:12.167 --> 00:00:13.400
opens at the
Lucille Lortel Theatre

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.701
here in New York
on November 2nd.

00:00:15.701 --> 00:00:17.734
Please welcome back to the show
one of our favorites,

00:00:17.734 --> 00:00:18.868
Colin Quinn.

00:00:18.868 --> 00:00:21.934
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.334
Welcome back!

00:00:33.334 --> 00:00:36.801
-No water?
What is it? COVID regulations?

00:00:36.801 --> 00:00:38.467
-Yeah.
Well, we have this one for you.

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:39.601
-Oh, thanks.
-Yeah.

00:00:39.601 --> 00:00:41.467
-Embarrassing.

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:43.767
I thought I scored an exposé.

00:00:43.767 --> 00:00:44.868
-You --

00:00:44.868 --> 00:00:46.400
-Before you start,
let's just say,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.734
nobody likes that you haven't
gained weight during COVID.

00:00:49.734 --> 00:00:53.501
It's rude to your guests,
and I don't like it.

00:00:53.501 --> 00:00:55.434
And I'm going on record.
But go on.

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.767
-Well, you take COVID
regulations very seriously.

00:00:57.767 --> 00:01:00.300
You are a man who
caught COVID twice.

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:01.467
And I'm glad to see
you're doing well.

00:01:01.467 --> 00:01:05.567
-Yes. Twice, I had COVID
and got the vaccine.

00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:09.534
And, yeah, that's how you know
it's not as bad as the plague,

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:11.267
because, you know,
in the Middle Ages,

00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:13.934
nobody was like,
"Hey, I got the plague twice.

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:17.601
I got it again.
Symptoms were mild."

00:01:17.601 --> 00:01:19.434
You know.

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:22.100
-Nobody -- When you got it,
was it still coronavirus?

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.100
'Cause I feel like
we stopped using that term.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.334
-Yeah, nobody says coronavirus.
It's only COVID now.

00:01:26.334 --> 00:01:28.834
Because, probably, the lobbyists
from Corona beer were like,

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:30.400
"Hey, what are you doing to us?"

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.167
And then -- like,
"Okay, we'll call it Delta."

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:33.167
They're like, "Fine."

00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:35.000
Then Delta is like, "What?
No, no."

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.767
And then COVID is like just one
angry Romanian guy.

00:01:37.767 --> 00:01:38.868
He's like, "My family name."

00:01:38.868 --> 00:01:40.000
They're like, "I'm sorry, sir,

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.834
but that's how it has to be."

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:44.567
-Do you feel -- How do you feel
like New York is handling it

00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:47.834
as often a spokesman for
the finer points of the city?

00:01:47.834 --> 00:01:49.000
-Yes. Thank you.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.167
I do consider myself
a spokesman for the city.

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:52.968
And I feel like I have to
keep doing that

00:01:52.968 --> 00:01:55.501
when people forget that
I'm a spokesman for the city.

00:01:55.501 --> 00:01:59.267
And New York
is obviously finished.

00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:03.934
What happened was, COVID --
Because when it flooded --

00:02:03.934 --> 00:02:05.667
When the subway flooded --

00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:08.234
When you saw freshwater
come into the subway,

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:10.534
it's not -- You know, urine,
anything would be fine,

00:02:10.534 --> 00:02:12.567
but freshwater is unnatural.

00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:15.868
And I realized it's because when
Bloomberg first started

00:02:15.868 --> 00:02:21.200
that no-smoking law in 2003,
that changed the --

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.801
We have a
very delicate ecosystem.

00:02:23.801 --> 00:02:26.601
And, you know, no floods could
get in before that,

00:02:26.601 --> 00:02:28.400
because this is the smoking
section of the country.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:30.667
-Right.
-People move here to smoke.

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.667
And no floods.

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:34.334
There was no COVID.
There was no disease.

00:02:34.334 --> 00:02:37.367
No airborne illnesses because
they couldn't get through.

00:02:37.367 --> 00:02:38.834
Even the sun barely got through.

00:02:38.834 --> 00:02:40.067
And...

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:41.367
-Well, because --
So the smoke was providing

00:02:41.367 --> 00:02:42.634
sort of like a defense layer.

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:43.767
-Yes.

00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:44.801
-Okay.

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:48.200
-And Times Square
was the rainforest.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.467
And it was like
a combination of spit --

00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:54.267
a delicate balance between spit,
body fluids, blood.

00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:58.267
And those bike lanes
created, like, a wind tunnel

00:02:58.267 --> 00:02:59.834
that allowed all the stuff in.

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:04.467
Those really were the downfall,
as you've seen, yeah.

00:03:04.467 --> 00:03:05.968
-One thing that people
have said --

00:03:05.968 --> 00:03:08.534
You know, and I think everybody
always has to preface it like,

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:09.667
"COVID was terrible.

00:03:09.667 --> 00:03:11.033
Obviously, it was horrifying.

00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:13.067
One thing like about it is
outdoor dining in the city,

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.100
and I hope it stays."

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.501
-Oh, of course,
because it's so --

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:19.167
People go, "Your table's ready.

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:22.501
It's that shed in the gutter.
Want to step right over there?"

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:25.067
You know...

00:03:25.067 --> 00:03:29.133
Like, the waiter is
sanding down your table.

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:32.000
There's a Con Ed worker
on a jackhammer

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.901
2 inches from your face.

00:03:33.901 --> 00:03:36.234
You're having
an anniversary dinner.

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:37.367
You're like, "You know,
when we first met --"

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:38.567
He's like,
"Lift your feet for a second."

00:03:38.567 --> 00:03:40.801
You're like, "What the..."

00:03:40.801 --> 00:03:43.067
Like, "Sparkling water or
fire hydrant, sir?

00:03:43.067 --> 00:03:45.033
What will it be?"

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:46.534
Plus, nothing's better than
when you try to get up

00:03:46.534 --> 00:03:48.000
and you forget the bike lane

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.467
and you almost get, like,
taken out by a delivery guy.

00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:54.033
But you can't say anything.
He's delivery, our heroes.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:57.667
Delivery. Apparently, been --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:57.667 --> 00:03:59.901
Alright, folks.
It's enough. We get it.

00:03:59.901 --> 00:04:02.400
We're all --
Medical people and delivery.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.267
The rest of us are nonessential.

00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:06.667
Like, that's a rude way
of phrasing it.

00:04:06.667 --> 00:04:07.968
Can't they just say,

00:04:07.968 --> 00:04:10.234
"People that were important
at a particular time"?

00:04:10.234 --> 00:04:13.467
And it's -- Yeah,
it's delivery and people --

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:16.467
You know, either you go to
medical school three years

00:04:16.467 --> 00:04:20.100
or you get an electric bike,
you have equal status.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.634
I'm sure the medical people
loved that one, you know?

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:26.067
They're like, "I studied
oncology at Stanford."

00:04:26.067 --> 00:04:27.601
It's like, "Hold on.
My pizza's here.

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:29.067
Wait a second.

00:04:29.067 --> 00:04:31.601
Oh, a first responder.
Thank you for your service."

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:33.634
You know?

00:04:33.634 --> 00:04:36.501
But it is -- It's weird, because
that's who's --

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:38.334
They've been -- Medical people
and restaurant people

00:04:38.334 --> 00:04:39.634
are interchangeable now.

00:04:39.634 --> 00:04:41.834
They've deputized -- The only
person that's taken

00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:42.968
my temperature past
year and a half

00:04:42.968 --> 00:04:44.000
is every hostess at
a restaurant.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.133
-Yep.

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.200
-Like, the waitress
is checking your vitals,

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.000
and the busboy will be back with

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:53.000
your blood work
in a few minutes.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:54.200
That's how I found out
I had COVID.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.367
I know you're going to think
I'm making it up.

00:04:55.367 --> 00:04:56.601
I get the phone call --
"You have COVID."

00:04:56.601 --> 00:04:58.100
I go, "Who is this?"
"Applebee's."

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.367
-No.

00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:03.033
-I said, "What should I do?"

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.234
They go, "We don't discuss it
over the phone.

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:05.601
Come down.

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:08.634
We'll talk about it over a
bottomless margarita for $8.99."

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:09.767
It sounds made up.

00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:11.567
It sounds like a bit.

00:05:11.567 --> 00:05:14.000
-It sounds like a bit.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.501
So, you know, some things
are going back to normal,

00:05:16.501 --> 00:05:21.067
and, yet, within this pandemic,
there have been some people

00:05:21.067 --> 00:05:23.834
who have sort of, like,
gone to the heights

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:26.033
and then all the way
lower than they started.

00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:27.400
Governor Cuomo.
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.133
Ellen and Governor Cuomo have
had a tough COVID.

00:05:31.133 --> 00:05:33.367
Yeah. Because Cuomo -- Remember,
everybody's like, "Oh, my God.

00:05:33.367 --> 00:05:35.400
Where he's been all my life,
this guy?

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:36.634
He should be
President of the United --

00:05:36.634 --> 00:05:37.834
The Man Who Would be King."

00:05:37.834 --> 00:05:42.701
And now he's
in a U-Haul on 17th,

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:44.934
going, "That escalated quickly."

00:05:44.934 --> 00:05:48.701
He's driving in circles around
Ulster -- Duchess County.

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:50.234
I said Ulster,
but then I went with Duchess.

00:05:50.234 --> 00:05:53.601
It's funnier.
I don't know why.

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:54.901
Last-minute decision.

00:05:54.901 --> 00:05:57.567
But, yeah, because he got --

00:05:57.567 --> 00:05:59.000
Remember, he's yelling
at us every day?

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.234
He got cocky.

00:06:00.234 --> 00:06:02.234
He's like,
"Hey, 6 feet distance.

00:06:02.234 --> 00:06:03.567
Don't touch anyone's face."

00:06:03.567 --> 00:06:08.467
All the stuff he couldn't
do with his staff, ironically.

00:06:08.467 --> 00:06:11.367
But of course,
we all overcorrect everything.

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:12.767
Now what is it?

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:15.200
Mario Cuomo Bridge -- They want
to rename Tappan Zee --

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:17.701
Mario Cuomo Bridge -- they want
to change it back

00:06:17.701 --> 00:06:20.834
because to punish Andrew Cuomo,
take his father's bridge,

00:06:20.834 --> 00:06:22.367
which is uncalled for.

00:06:22.367 --> 00:06:24.567
And it's not going to
change anything.

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:26.100
He's still going to be
Andrew Cuomo.

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:27.501
-Right.
-It doesn't even make sense.

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:29.801
They should leave
the name Mario Cuomo Bridge

00:06:29.801 --> 00:06:34.968
and make him change his name to,
you know, Andrew Tappan Zee.

00:06:34.968 --> 00:06:36.167
-There you go.
-You know.

00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:37.767
-And then Mario Cuomo
doesn't get punished.

00:06:37.767 --> 00:06:38.901
-That's right.

00:06:38.901 --> 00:06:40.934
-"Last Best Hope"
is the title of your show.

00:06:40.934 --> 00:06:43.901
You have not been --
I think it's safe to say

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:46.367
you haven't been --
You've been very clear-eyed

00:06:46.367 --> 00:06:49.501
about this country, but maybe
not particularly optimistic.

00:06:49.501 --> 00:06:51.868
Why did you come up with the
title "Last Best Hope"?

00:06:51.868 --> 00:06:54.601
-Well, because that's
what Abraham Lincoln said

00:06:54.601 --> 00:06:56.300
right before the Civil War.

00:06:56.300 --> 00:07:00.300
And he said,
"We're the last best hope."

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:01.734
I forget exactly how
he phrased it,

00:07:01.734 --> 00:07:04.634
but -- which I should know since
it's the title of the show.

00:07:04.634 --> 00:07:05.901
You know?

00:07:05.901 --> 00:07:08.868
But, yeah, I feel like,
look, this country's --

00:07:08.868 --> 00:07:11.467
I think everyone's shocked that
we're not in a civil war

00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:12.734
right now, never mind --

00:07:12.734 --> 00:07:15.400
And you know, civil war is
the worst kind of war

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:17.267
to have because you have to see
each other afterwards.

00:07:17.267 --> 00:07:18.968
-Yeah.
-So it's pretty awkward.

00:07:18.968 --> 00:07:20.267
You're like, "Oh, my God.
Is that Missouri?

00:07:20.267 --> 00:07:24.767
Didn't we bomb..."

00:07:24.767 --> 00:07:29.501
But, yeah, I do feel like
this -- I love this country,

00:07:29.501 --> 00:07:34.567
but I think we should sell,
because, you know,

00:07:34.567 --> 00:07:39.133
now is the time to just --
HGTV is very popular.

00:07:39.133 --> 00:07:40.734
The Founding Fathers and
"Property Brothers."

00:07:40.734 --> 00:07:42.300
We knock down the Rockies,
open concept.

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:44.000
You know how these shows go.

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:46.300
Got the double sinks,
the Atlantic and Pacific.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:48.000
Yeah, I went through
the whole thing with the bit.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:52.400
And...
[ Laughter ]

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.467
Nice backsplash,
the Grand Canyon.

00:07:55.467 --> 00:07:57.234
You know,
here's the thing about a bit.

00:07:57.234 --> 00:07:58.501
I finish them.

00:07:58.501 --> 00:08:00.434
They're not always hilarious,
but I finish them.

00:08:00.434 --> 00:08:01.734
-You don't bail on it.
You finish the bit.

00:08:01.734 --> 00:08:04.200
-No, I bail.

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.868
Flip the Louisiana Purchase.

00:08:07.868 --> 00:08:09.434
And change our motto.

00:08:09.434 --> 00:08:11.567
"America -- love it or list it."

00:08:11.567 --> 00:08:13.300
Now it's done.
-Perfect.

00:08:13.300 --> 00:08:14.367
-Now I'm done.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:14.367 --> 00:08:15.734
Thank you, folks.

00:08:15.734 --> 00:08:18.200
-Colin Quinn, it is always a
pleasure to see you, sir.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:19.234
Thank you for making
the time for us.

00:08:19.234 --> 00:08:20.300
-Thank you.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.467
-"Colin Quinn:
The Last Best Hope"

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:24.734
opens at the
Lucille Lortel Theatre tomorrow.

00:08:24.734 --> 00:08:27.133
For more information,
go to colinquinn.com.

